    {
      "op": "replace",
      "path": "/graph/inferences/3/argument/0/text",
      "value": "Fix arbitrary r>0, let J=[r/2,3r/2], and choose compact C from H003 for J. Put C*=C∪{0}, which is compact and nonempty; outside C* we still have u=q throughout J. Thus e_t is continuous on C*×J. By M005, C*×J is compact. Apply M019 to the continuous real function (x,τ)↦|e_t(x,τ)| on this compact set; it is bounded above, so there exists a finite nonnegative K with |e_t(x,τ)|≤K on C*×J; record this existence as F001. Define b(x)=K·1_{C*}(x). Outside C*, e_t=0 by smoothness and constancy of u on the open spatial complement; hence |e_t(x,τ)|≤b(x) for every x and τ∈J. By N001 every compact set has finite Lebesgue measure, so C* is measurable and has finite measure. M008 makes the constant K integrable on C* and M009 makes b integrable on ℝⁿ. Define F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ) and Fprime(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ). Smoothness implies these functions are strongly measurable; N001 gives F(r) integrable, and Fprime(r) strongly measurable. The interval (r/2,3r/2) is a neighborhood of r. The pointwise parameter derivative of F is Fprime by smoothness, and the bound by b holds for every x and every τ in this neighborhood. Thus M006 applies and yields differentiability of E at r with E′(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx. Since r was arbitrary, the output follows."
    },
